    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "c2596f0e-7ddc-3d58-b1e0-f84a90ca74db",
                "word": "Chartism",
                "pos": "name",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "1659d739-97cf-4071-ab2a-b2f4011c1125",
                        "entry_id": "c2596f0e-7ddc-3d58-b1e0-f84a90ca74db",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "Chartisms"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "ffb123c7-41de-3b85-83b3-246dd72537ed",
                        "entry_id": "c2596f0e-7ddc-3d58-b1e0-f84a90ca74db",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A working-class movement for political and social reform in the United Kingdom during the mid-19th century."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "historical"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "7ab2b0ca-fc9a-4c8a-af23-454e59141838",
                                "sense_id": "ffb123c7-41de-3b85-83b3-246dd72537ed",
                                "text": "An insincere world; a godless untruth of a world! It is out of this, as I consider, that the whole tribe of social pestilences, French Revolutions, Chartisms, and what not, have derived their being,—their chief necessity to be."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4164b73b-6e31-479c-85f6-f7ab4e6bc2e5",
                                "sense_id": "ffb123c7-41de-3b85-83b3-246dd72537ed",
                                "text": "[…] when she married the young clergyman, and went to settle in that smoky hole Turley; a very nest of Chartism and Atheism, in a part of the country which all the decent families had had to leave for years."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4a23abc0-042a-4274-804b-a224495efc69",
                                "sense_id": "ffb123c7-41de-3b85-83b3-246dd72537ed",
                                "text": "Chartism therefore, though a genuine popular movement, was incomplete in its aims and knowledge; the time was not yet come and it could not triumph openly; but it would be a mistake to say that it failed utterly: […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8c0f3ec3-2054-49cd-939c-2772115b684c",
                                "sense_id": "ffb123c7-41de-3b85-83b3-246dd72537ed",
                                "text": "The dangers of this revolutionary ferment were grave; even in England Chartism was rampant—a sinister movement, which might at any moment upset the Constitution and abolish the Monarchy."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "dfcf59f3-dbb3-4c04-9316-ee2ba8018a6c",
                                "sense_id": "ffb123c7-41de-3b85-83b3-246dd72537ed",
                                "text": "When Owenism and Chartism had burned themselves out, England had become poorer by that substance out of which the Anglo-Saxon ideal of a free society could have been built up for centuries to come."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
